diff --git a/Lab3_Notes b/Lab3_Notes
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d2e730a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Lab3_Notes
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+Implement a SLEEP SYS Call
+
+- Check the Alarm Function
+- Put processess to the Sleep List then go and trigger a function using Alarm Function every second, then this function shud go and
+check the sleep list and then check whether any processes has finished sleeping - time elapsed > sleep time - then release it from the list
+- modify the scheduler to ignore any program that is in the sleep list
+
+
+What I did:
+
+- The timer always called the Callback function in the alarm.h and alarm.cc files
+- Made a custom sorted list with custom compare function to handle sleeping threads
+- Sleeping thread is a new class that is created side by side alarm class
+- The WaitUntil function (that is declared inside alarm.h ) is now implemented in alarm.cc where the  waituntil function makes an entry in the sleepingthread sorted list using the amount ( ticks ) provided
+wakeuptime for the thread is set as currenttime + ticks ... then the Thread::Sleep function is called so the Thread now goes into Sleep Mode....
+- Now everytime the callback is called by the timer, we use the Alarm:Callback function  check whether the front queue ( least wakeuptime -> because its sorted like that ) has a wakeuptime that is les that current time
+then we move it to the ReadyToRun list in the sheduler
+- Hence the BLOCKED thread is now put into ready to run
+- Also implemented Thread:Sleep2 because sir told us to ...
+
diff --git a/Lab4_Notes b/Lab4_Notes
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..083f974
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Lab4_Notes
@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+Initialize pipe syscall
+it will take two variables as input : x and y 
+it will assign file descriptors to both x and y 
+
+then x will write to a buffer and y will read through a buffer (will be shared) it will be called a pipe buffer
+so we will then have to implement a Write and Read syscall then - which will both read and write to buffers differently for pipe and normally otherwise
+after we do Exec after doing pipe, Exec will inherit both pointers
+
+
+parent will write using one of the fd and child will read using the other fd yeah?
+
+
+Main BluePrint:
+
+int fd[2];
+Pipe(fd);        // fd[0] = read end, fd[1] = write end
+// fd[0] and fd[1] are just integers (file descriptors)
+
+Exec(cmd1);      // child inherits both fd[0] and fd[1]
+// cmd1 writes to fd[1]
+// cmd2 reads from fd[0]
+
+
+What you need to implement:
+
+PipeBuffer struct in kernel
+A file descriptor table per process (array of pointers, some point to files, some to pipes)
+SC_Pipe syscall — allocates PipeBuffer, returns two fd's
+Modify SC_Read and SC_Write to check if fd is a pipe fd and route accordingly
+Inherit fd table on Exec
\ No newline at end of file
